problem_statement:
Interest management never engages: players are never re-homed, so every subscriber receives the whole world
**Version:** asobi 0.46.0, asobi_lua 0.0.0, image `ghcr.io/widgrensit/asobi_lua:latest`
(revision `2c1ccb45facfe91d31d0d9b3dec59c6ad221aee5`, built 2026-07-31, i.e. with
the fix for #246).

## Summary

[The world server guide](https://asobi.hexdocs.pm/world-server.html) documents
interest management:

> Players only receive updates from zones they can see (interest management).

with `view_radius` controlling the scope — "subscribe to 1 zone in each
direction (3x3 = 9 zones)", default 1.

That never happens in a running world. A player's zone subscription is fixed at
join and never changes, because **nothing in the live input path calls
`asobi_world_server:move_player/3`** — its only callers in the tree are tests.
Every player therefore stays subscribed to the zone they spawned in, every
player's entity is written into that one zone's entity map, and every subscriber
receives all of it regardless of distance.

The re-homing machinery itself is complete and correct. It is simply never
reached from a WebSocket client.

## Reproduction

Self-contained, Node 22+, no dependencies (script at the end of this issue).
Two guest clients spawn in the same zone; one walks 50 zones east; both report
at 15 Hz like a real client. It exits 0 once distant entities are filtered, so
it doubles as a regression check.

```
$ node interest-management.mjs
world 019fb9b3-73a1-732d-a091-a451076750b4
both spawned in zone 1000,1000
same zone   -> Ada receives Bob: true

Ada in zone 1000,1000 | Bob in zone 1050,1000 | 50 zones apart
Ada still receives Bob:        true (at 16800,16000)
props Ada receives from >2 zones away: 8
entities held -- Ada: 16  Bob: 16

FAIL: no interest filtering, every subscriber gets everything
```

Both clients hold the identical set of 16 entities: two players and the props of
both players' zones. With `view_radius = 1` and 50 zones of separation, neither
should see anything of the other. (The props come from the game script this was
found with; the minimal script below reports 0 of them and still leaks the
player.)

Run it against a freshly started server: a world left over from a previous run
can answer `world.find_or_create` and then never send `world.joined`.

The world it was run against, reduced to the parts that matter:

```lua
-- lua/world.lua, registered as { spawns = "world.lua" } in lua/config.lua,
-- which also needs guest_auth = true
game_type   = "world"
match_size  = 1
max_players = 16
grid_size   = 2000        -- 2000 x 2000 zones of 16 units
zone_size   = 16
view_radius = 1
lazy_zones  = true

local SPAWN = grid_size * zone_size / 2   -- 16000, the middle of the map

function init(config) return {} end
function generate_world(seed, config) return {} end
function spawn_position(player_id, state) return { x = SPAWN, y = SPAWN } end
function join(player_id, state) return state end
function leave(player_id, state) return state end
function post_tick(tick_n, state) return state end
function zone_tick(entities, zone_state) return entities, zone_state end

function handle_input(player_id, input, entities)
    if not input or input.kind ~= "move" then return entities end
    local e = entities[player_id] or {}
    e.type = "player"
    e.x = tonumber(input.x) or SPAWN
    e.y = tonumber(input.y) or SPAWN
    e.name = input.name or e.name
    entities[player_id] = e
    return entities
end
```

## Where the chain breaks

```
world.input (WS)
  -> asobi_ws_handler.erl:618   asobi_zone:player_input(ZonePid, PlayerId, InputData)
                                ZonePid = maps:get(zone_pid, SState)  -- bound at join
  -> asobi_zone                 applies the input to its own entity map
  X                             nothing calls asobi_world_server:move_player/3
```

`asobi_ws_handler` routes `world.input` to the zone pid cached in the player
session, and that is the end of it:

```erlang
%% src/ws/asobi_ws_handler.erl:618
handle_message(
    #{~"type" := ~"world.input", ~"payload" := Payload},
    #{session := SessionPid} = State
) when SessionPid =/= undefined ->
    ...
            case maps:get(zone_pid, SState, undefined) of
                ...
                ZonePid ->
                    ...
                    asobi_zone:player_input(ZonePid, PlayerId, InputData),
```

`asobi_zone` is given `world_server_pid` (`asobi_zone.erl:123`), stores it in
its state (`:155`) and passes it into the callback context (`:194`) — but never
sends it anything. Nothing in the module references the world server beyond
carrying that pid around.

`asobi_world_server:move_player/3` is the entry point that would fix all of
this, and it is exported:

```erlang
%% src/world/asobi_world_server.erl:69
-spec move_player(pid(), binary(), {number(), number()}) -> ok.
move_player(Pid, PlayerId, NewPos) ->
    gen_statem:cast(Pid, {move_player, PlayerId, NewPos}).
```

Its callers, in the whole repository:

```
$ grep -rn "asobi_world_server:move_player" src/ test/
test/asobi_world_zone_integration_tests.erl:97:    asobi_world_server:move_player(Pid, ~"p1", {250.0, 250.0}),
test/asobi_world_zone_integration_tests.erl:124:   asobi_world_server:move_player(Pid, ~"p1", {250.0, 250.0}),
test/prop_zone_invariants.erl:157:                 asobi_world_server:move_player(Pid, P, NewPos),
```

Tests only. Which is why the behaviour is covered by the suite and still absent
[HIDDEN]

## The rest of the pipeline is correct

Everything downstream of `move_player/3` is implemented and consistent:

[HIDDEN] `remove_player_from_zones/2`, `place_player/3`,
  [HIDDEN] and stores
  the new ring in `player_zones`;
- `subscribe_interest_zones/4` and `unsubscribe_interest_zones/3` (`:845`,
  `:859`) do the zone subscription bookkeeping;
- [HIDDEN] `{asobi_message, {world_zone_changed, ZonePid}}`
  to the player process, and `asobi_player_session` already handles it:

```erlang
%% src/players/asobi_player_session.erl:93
handle_info({asobi_message, {world_zone_changed, ZonePid}}, State) ->
    {noreply, State#{zone_pid => ZonePid}};
```

So both ends exist — the session knows how to be re-pointed at a new zone, and
the world server knows how to re-point it. The wire between the input path and
`move_player/3` is the only missing piece.

## Impact

- `view_radius` has no observable effect; a client cannot rely on the server to
  scope what it receives and has to cull locally, which is cosmetic only — the
  data still crosses the wire.
- Bandwidth and per-tick delta size scale with the total number of players in
  the world, not with the number nearby, which is the property zoning exists to
  provide.
- Zone processes do not reflect where players actually are: one zone owns every
  player and, for a Lua game that seeds props from player positions, every
  player's props too. `zone_idle_timeout` and `cold_tick_divisor` are measured
  against a population that never moves.
- Nothing corrects the misplacement afterwards.
  `asobi_zone:transfer_out_of_bounds_npcs/1` (`asobi_zone.erl:621`) matches only
  `#{type := ~"npc"}`, so entities of any other type sit in the entity map of a
  zone that does not own their coordinates for as long as the script keeps them
  — in the run above, 8 props belonging to zone 1050,1000 are held and
  broadcast by zone 1000,1000.

## Expected

A `world.input` that moves a player across a zone boundary should re-home them:
unsubscribe the zones that left the ring, subscribe the ones that entered, send
removals for the entities of the dropped zones, and route subsequent input to
the new zone process — the behaviour `handle_move/3` already implements.

## Repro script

<details>
<summary><code>interest-management.mjs</code></summary>

```js
// Repro for the interest-management issue: two players 50 zones apart still
// receive each other's entities and each other's props.
//
// Self-contained. Node 22+ (uses the built-in WebSocket and fetch), no deps:
//
//     node interest-management.mjs [host:port]
//
// The server needs guest_auth = true in the mode manifest and a >= 32-byte
// ASOBI_GUEST_VERIFIER_PEPPER. It also needs a world mode whose script keeps a
// `player` entity per connected client and a grid big enough to walk 50 zones
// across; the issue this ships with has a minimal one.

import crypto from "node:crypto";

const HOST = process.argv[2] || "localhost:8080";
const MODE = process.env.MODE || "spawns";

// Must match zone_size in the world script, and spawn_position.
const ZONE_SIZE = Number(process.env.ZONE_SIZE || 16);
const SPAWN = Number(process.env.SPAWN || 16000);
// 50 zones east: far outside any plausible view_radius.
const FAR = SPAWN + 50 * ZONE_SIZE;

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const b64 = (n) => crypto.randomBytes(n).toString("base64");
const zoneOf = (v) => Math.floor(v / ZONE_SIZE);

class Client {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    this.entities = new Map();
    this.joined = false;
    this.placed = false;
    this.cid = 0;
    this.seq = 0;
    this.x = SPAWN;
    this.y = SPAWN;
  }

  async start() {
    // A fresh device pair per client, or both sign in as the same player.
    const res = await fetch(`http://${HOST}/api/v1/auth/guest`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ device_id: b64(16), device_secret: b64(32) }),
    });
    const auth = await res.json();
    if (!auth.access_token) throw new Error(`${this.name}: auth failed ${res.status} ${JSON.stringify(auth)}`);
    this.playerId = auth.player_id;

    this.ws = new WebSocket(`ws://${HOST}/ws`);
    await new Promise((ok, fail) => {
      this.ws.onopen = ok;
      this.ws.onerror = fail;
    });
    this.ws.onmessage = (e) => this.onMessage(JSON.parse(e.data));
    this.send("session.connect", { token: auth.access_token });

    for (let i = 0; i < 100 && !this.placed; i++) await sleep(100);
    if (!this.placed) throw new Error(`${this.name}: never received a spawn placement`);
  }

  send(type, payload) {
    this.ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type, payload, cid: String(++this.cid) }));
  }

  onMessage({ type, payload = {} }) {
    if (type === "session.connected") {
      this.send("world.find_or_create", { mode: MODE });
    } else if (type === "world.joined") {
      this.joined = true;
      this.worldId = payload.world_id;
    } else if (type === "world.tick") {
      for (const u of payload.updates || []) {
        if (u.op === "r") {
          this.entities.delete(u.id);
          continue;
        }
        // "u" carries changed fields only.
        const prev = u.op === "u" ? this.entities.get(u.id) || {} : {};
        this.entities.set(u.id, { ...prev, ...u });
        if (u.id === this.playerId && !this.placed) this.placed = true;
      }
    } else if (type.startsWith("world.") && !this.joined && payload.world_id) {
      this.send("world.join", { world_id: payload.world_id });
    }
  }

  // The real client reports at 15 Hz whether or not it moved.
  moveTo(x, y) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }

  report() {
    this.ws.send(JSON.stringify({
      type: "world.input",
      payload: { kind: "move", x: this.x, y: this.y, yaw: 0, name: this.name, seq: ++this.seq },
    }));
  }

  players() {
    return [...this.entities.values()].filter((e) => e.type === "player");
  }

  props() {
    return [...this.entities.values()].filter((e) => e.type !== "player");
  }
}

const ada = new Client("Ada");
const bob = new Client("Bob");

await ada.start();
await bob.start();
const beat = setInterval(() => {
  ada.report();
  bob.report();
}, 1000 / 15);
await sleep(1500);

console.log(`world ${ada.worldId}`);
console.log(`both spawned in zone ${zoneOf(SPAWN)},${zoneOf(SPAWN)}`);
console.log(`same zone   -> Ada receives Bob: ${ada.entities.has(bob.playerId)}`);

// Bob walks east in steps, so the server sees a continuous path.
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  bob.moveTo(SPAWN + ((FAR - SPAWN) * i) / 10, SPAWN);
  await sleep(300);
}
await sleep(3000);

const adaZone = zoneOf(ada.x);
const bobZone = zoneOf(bob.x);
const bobEntity = ada.entities.get(bob.playerId);
const distantProps = ada.props().filter((e) => Math.abs(zoneOf(e.x) - adaZone) > 2);

console.log(`\nAda in zone ${adaZone},${zoneOf(ada.y)} | Bob in zone ${bobZone},${zoneOf(bob.y)} | ${Math.abs(bobZone - adaZone)} zones apart`);
console.log(`Ada still receives Bob:        ${!!bobEntity}${bobEntity ? ` (at ${bobEntity.x},${bobEntity.y})` : ""}`);
console.log(`props Ada receives from >2 zones away: ${distantProps.length}`);
console.log(`entities held -- Ada: ${ada.entities.size}  Bob: ${bob.entities.size}`);

const leaked = !!bobEntity || distantProps.length > 0;
console.log(`\n${leaked ? "FAIL: no interest filtering, every subscriber gets everything" : "PASS: distant entities are filtered"}`);

clearInterval(beat);
ada.ws.close();
bob.ws.close();
process.exit(leaked ? 1 : 0);
```

</details>

### Additional contract requirements

This addendum supplements the issue statement with additional behaviors that the
hidden suite also requires; any compliant implementation must satisfy all of the
following.

## Crossing failure robustness

When a player move crosses a zone boundary and the destination zone cannot be
started or acquired (its creation fails, e.g. the world has reached its zone
limit), the failed move is a no-op:

- The world must not crash, hang, or terminate. It keeps running and keeps all
  of its players tracked (the player count is unchanged).
- The player remains owned by their current zone: the world's membership
  records for that player still point at that zone, and the player's entity in
  that zone is completely untouched, including every script-maintained field
  beyond position and type.

The same robustness must also hold when the destination zone becomes
unavailable at a later internal hand-off step of the same move — for example a
first acquisition of the destination succeeds and a subsequent placement
attempt fails after some legs of the hand-off have already run. In that case:

- The world must not crash, hang, or terminate. It keeps running, keeps the
  player on its roster, and keeps a tracked position for the player that stays
  inside the world grid bounds.

## Result of a successful live-input crossing

When a live input carrying a new position causes a session player to cross into
a different zone:

- The player becomes owned by the destination zone: the world re-registers the
  player's zone membership, refreshes the membership records and interest
  subscriptions to surround the destination zone, and notifies the player
  process of its new owning zone so that subsequent input is routed there.
- A later live input sent to the destination zone is accepted normally by that
  zone: it is applied to the player's entity there.
- If the destination zone is created on demand by the crossing, it must
  continue receiving world ticks afterwards; a player rehomed by an input must
  not end up in a zone that stops ticking.

Any player-specific fields stored on the player's entity beyond its position
and type must be preserved across the crossing.

